fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1018", description: some(value: "Value::OpRef must not be ViewHead::Opaque. MEASURED (probe, prelude KB, OpRef{op, dict: none, named: none}): head = Opaque; views_structurally_equal(a, a) = FALSE — an OpRef is not equal to ITSELF; two OpRefs naming DIFFERENT ops share one goal_fingerprint and the key is not opaque_free. That is the exact symptom pair that made Expr::SetLit the wrong thing to leave Opaque (WI-1014), and WI-815 made it a soundness surface: an over-dedup DROPS an answer in seen_goals and DROPS A FACT in value_fact_dedup. WHY THE EXISTING JUSTIFICATION DOES NOT APPLY: OpRef sits in the same Opaque group as Stream / Map / Cell / Substitution / Requirement / Closure, whose stated reason (Value::Relation's doc) is 'a native carrier is not structural data' — an arena handle whose identity is a slot and whose content mutates. An OpRef is NOT a handle: it is {op: Symbol, dict: Option<RequirementHandle>, named: Option<Symbol>}, a pure value whose op and named halves are structural. Only dict is a runtime environment. THE DESIGN QUESTION, which is why this is a ticket and not an arm: what head? It must NOT be ViewHead::Ref(op) or a nullary Functor{op} — WI-436's functor_view_head note relies on 'an op-as-value is a Value::OpRef, never a Term::Ref, so no op/eta case depends on the Ref-vs-Fn shape', and conflating them would make an eta'd op unify with a bare constructor reference of the same name. Options: (a) a new ViewHead::OpRef(Symbol) variant — no conflation, gives discrim a concrete key, but every ViewHead matcher must decide about it (DiscrimKey, fingerprint_into, views_structurally_equal, unify); (b) a reflect-style wrapper twin Fn{Function.op_ref, named:[op, named]} — reuses the existing machinery but needs a declared reflect form and a term twin that nothing builds today; (c) keep Opaque but give ViewHead a payload-BEARING opaque so distinct handles at least key distinctly. DECIDE (b)/(a) against whether a term twin exists at all: if nothing lowers an OpRef to a term, WI-425's 'agree with your twin' rule dictates no shape, and the argument rests only on self-equality and key distinctness. CHECK THE SIBLINGS in the same group while here — Closure / Map / Cell / Stream ARE arena handles and their Opaque is sound, but Substitution and Requirement should be re-examined on the same test (is it equal to itself? do two distinct ones share a key?). CONSEQUENCE: wi815_head's opaque subject was briefly pointed at OpRef under WI-1014 on the false premise that it is opaque by nature; it is now Value::FactRef, whose OWN DOC says it 'never exposes a resident RuleId', so a structural view would contradict the type. That subject is unaffected by this ticket."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T16:01:33Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1018", author: "user", content: "SUPERSEDED BY WI-1019, which is the MECHANISM this ticket only described an instance of. This item framed the question as 'what head should an OpRef get' and listed a new ViewHead variant / a wrapper twin / a payload-bearing opaque as options. The better answer is none of those three as posed: GENERATE an entity representation for the native carrier, reusing the wrapped_expr_head shape table, so equality and keying and indexing all come from the structural machinery instead of a per-carrier decision. ALSO CORRECTING THIS ITEM'S OWN CONSEQUENCE NOTE: it said wi815_head's subject 'is now Value::FactRef, whose OWN DOC says it never exposes a resident RuleId, so a structural view would contradict the type. That subject is unaffected by this ticket.' MEASURED, both halves are wrong. FactRef behaves identically to OpRef — head Opaque, views_structurally_equal(a, a) = FALSE, two distinct rows sharing one goal_fingerprint. The doc's 'opaque' means ENCAPSULATED (do not hand a RuleId to client code), not STRUCTURE-FREE, and ViewHead is internal machinery — keying on identity exposes nothing to anthill. Two subject choices in a row (OpRef, then FactRef) were made by reading docs instead of running the two probes, and both were wrong the same way. The subject IS affected, and WI-1019 must decide it.", at: "2026-08-05T13:26:23Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1018", author: "user", content: "RESOLVED BY WI-1019 (delivered). Value::OpRef is no longer Opaque: it views as Functor{anthill.realization.runtime.OpRef, 0, N} with named op/dict/named, so equality, goal_fingerprint keying, discrim indexing and unification all come from the structural machinery.\n\nTHE THREE OPTIONS THIS TICKET POSED WERE ALL WRONG, AND SO WAS THE SUPERSEDING NOTE'S FOURTH. (a) a new ViewHead variant, (b) a wrapper twin Fn{Function.op_ref, ...}, (c) a payload-bearing opaque — and the follow-up proposed generating an entity via the wrapped_expr_head shape table. The actual answer needed no new form at all: anthill.realization.runtime already declares sort OpRef and sort Dictionary, constructor-less, with accessor builtins registered since WI-577, owned by docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md §2. The view heads under the sort that was already there. Both this ticket and its follow-up asked 'what head should we invent' when the question was 'what is this type already called'.\n\nTHIS TICKET'S 'CHECK THE SIBLINGS' INSTRUCTION IS WHAT FOUND THE REST, and it pointed the opposite way from its own prediction. It grouped Requirement with the arena handles and asked for it to be re-examined; re-examined, a requirement slot is {functor, requirements, refcount}, never mutated after alloc, and requirement-dictionaries.md §2.1 already called both OpRef and Dictionary 'immutable resolved values — no identity/mutation hazard (unlike Cell)'. Requirement is now structural too, which is what removes the dict child's Opaque and with it the last reason to keep a second equality path.\n\nAND THE CONSEQUENCE NOTE'S CORRECTION WAS ITSELF HALF WRONG. It said 'MEASURED, both halves are wrong. FactRef behaves identically to OpRef.' The SYMPTOMS were identical — Opaque head, not equal to itself, two rows sharing a key — but identical symptoms are not an identical cause, and that inference is what the correction got wrong. FactRef stays Opaque, by nature. The discriminator (the user's) is what the reference is spelled IN: an OpRef names its target with a Symbol, which has a Value carrier and denotes the same op to every reader; a FactRef locates its row by a private slot index (RuleId / RowKey) with no Value carrier at all. A shape to present vs an identity to hold. So wi815_head's subject IS unaffected after all, exactly as this ticket originally said and as its correction denied — and its doc comment was right the whole time.\n\nThe ViewHead-vs-surface half of the correction DOES hold and is now written into 005: view-level identity exposes nothing to anthill, while 'sort FactRef = ?' is a statement about the surface. Both hold at once; they just do not add up to a structural head here.", at: "2026-08-05T16:01:17Z")
